    "title": "Two charged in Canton home explosion - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Danielle Langenfeld ,\n11 hours ago\nCANTON , Ohio (WJW) — Two men have been charged in connection with a home explosion in Canton that witnesses said could be felt for miles.\nAccording to a media release, Justin Bruce, 41, and Michael Mullins, 42, have been charged with\nbreaking and entering and nine counts of aggravated arson.\nCanton mom of 3 tries to rebuild after explosion On July 30, witnesses told FOX 8 News that the sound of an explosion at 1617 Bonnot Place NE, woke them up shortly before 4 a.m.\nCanton Fire Division Chief Steve Henderson told FOX 8 News the explosion could be felt 2 miles away . Firefighters said at least five additional houses were damaged, and 23 people were displaced.\nJelly Roll makes career announcement The home where the explosion happened was empty and on sale at the time, according to Canton Fire.\nFOX 8 FOX 8 FOX 8 On home video shared with FOX 8, a badly injured man who ended up in a front yard across the street from the home is overheard saying, “I was trying to get copper. I lit a lighter to light a cigarette and boom.”\nAt the time, Division Fire Chief Steve Henderson said he had also heard the alleged confession, but as of Friday the investigation was still open.\nCredit: Canton police Credit: Dalton Thompson YouTube According to Canton fire officials, both suspects are currently at Akron Children’s Hospital’s Burn Unit, where they will continue receiving treatment for related injuries.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to Fox 8 Cleveland WJW.\n9",
    "summary": "According to a media release, Justin Bruce, 41, and Michael Mullins, 42, have been charged with breaking and entering and nine counts of aggravated arson. Jelly Roll makes career announcement The home where the explosion happened was empty and on sale at the time, according to Canton Fire. I lit a lighter to light a cigarette and boom.” At the time, Division Fire Chief Steve Henderson said he had also heard the alleged confession, but as of Friday the investigation was still open.",
